APS-020 — Future Evolution

Document ID: APS-020
Title: Future Evolution
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Constitutional

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Evolution Framework (CPEF) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Evolution Framework establishes the principles, governance mechanisms, compatibility rules, and extension processes that govern the long-term evolution of the Atlas Policy Specification while preserving constitutional stability, interoperability, semantic integrity, and backward compatibility.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Specification evolution
Compatibility preservation
Extension mechanisms
Deprecation
Constitutional amendments
Version governance
Innovation framework
Long-term stewardship

This specification applies to every future revision of APS.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Evolution is the governed process through which the Atlas Policy Specification changes while preserving constitutional principles, semantic integrity, interoperability, and historical continuity.

Evolution changes the specification.

It SHALL NOT invalidate previously conforming implementations without explicit constitutional revision.

4. Objectives

The Policy Evolution Framework SHALL:

Preserve constitutional stability.
Enable controlled innovation.
Support backward compatibility.
Encourage extensibility.
Preserve interoperability.
Enable international adoption.
Maintain governance integrity.
Ensure long-term sustainability.
5. Evolution Principles

Every revision of APS SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Constitutional Stability

The constitutional principles established by APS SHALL remain stable across revisions unless amended through the constitutional amendment process.

Semantic Preservation

Future revisions SHALL preserve the meaning of existing canonical concepts unless explicitly superseded.

Backward Compatibility

Backward compatibility SHOULD be maintained whenever reasonably achievable.

Breaking changes SHALL require a new major specification version.

Explicit Deprecation

Deprecated concepts SHALL remain documented until formally retired.

Silent removal SHALL NOT occur.

Open Extensibility

Organizations MAY extend APS provided extensions preserve canonical semantics.

Transparent Governance

Every specification revision SHALL be publicly documented and traceable.

6. Evolution Categories

Atlas recognizes the following categories of specification evolution.

Category	Description
Editorial	Clarifications only
Informative	Non-normative additions
Minor	Backward-compatible normative additions
Major	Breaking normative changes
Constitutional	Changes to foundational governance principles

Each revision SHALL declare its category.

7. Extension Model

Extensions MAY introduce:

New policy families
New governance profiles
Domain vocabularies
Industry-specific modules
Regional governance profiles
Additional conformance profiles

Extensions SHALL NOT redefine existing canonical semantics.

8. Deprecation Model

Every deprecated element SHALL define:

Deprecated element
Deprecation rationale
Replacement guidance
Compatibility period
Planned retirement version

Deprecated elements SHALL remain documented until retirement.

9. Amendment Process

Constitutional amendments SHALL require:

Formal proposal
Technical review
Governance review
Impact assessment
Public documentation
Approval by the constitutional governance authority

Amendments SHALL preserve historical traceability.

10. Compatibility Rules

Future APS revisions SHALL explicitly declare:

Backward compatibility
Forward compatibility
Migration requirements
Conformance impacts
Interoperability impacts

Compatibility SHALL be objectively assessable.

11. Innovation Framework

Atlas encourages innovation through:

Experimental profiles
Reference implementations
Research extensions
Academic collaboration
Industry working groups
Open governance proposals

Experimental work SHALL remain clearly distinguished from constitutional specifications.

12. Stewardship

